comments.body:
In commit "rpc: Return proper error messages from getrawtransaction" (fa5b2f13da03d295ab624190f0d393b13da5b929)

Not blocking, but the race this comment describes could be closed by checking again after the read fails. The old order had no race: it checked `BLOCK_HAVE_DATA` only after the read had failed, so a block pruned in between was reported as "Block not available". With the check moved before the read, the same race now produces "I/O error reading block data". Keeping this up-front check for the better messages and re-running it when `GetTransaction` throws would report pruned data correctly in both cases. For example:

```cpp
CTransactionRef tx;
try {
    tx = GetTransaction(blockindex, node.mempool.get(), txid, chainman.m_blockman, hash_block);
} catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    if (blockindex) WITH_LOCK(::cs_main, CheckBlockDataAvailability(chainman.m_blockman, *blockindex, /*check_for_undo=*/false));
    throw;
}
```

Alternatively, `GetTransaction` could check `BLOCK_HAVE_DATA` itself after a failed `ReadBlock` and only throw the I/O error if the flag is still set. Either would fit in a follow-up.

Update: the Ralph review [also flags this](https://review.fish.foo/traces/35728-fa87067c397814f79566f8e1b4f407745e3e1f8c-1695-11.html#:~:text=Distinguish%20a%20pruning%20race%20from%20a%20block%20read%20error).

